<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-cumulative-frequency-curves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Cumulative Frequency Curves</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        3.0 Statistics and Probability
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        3.1 Statistics II
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        3.1 Statistics III
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p component="core">
                        Draw a cumulative frequency curve for grouped data
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Draw a cumulative frequency curve for a set of data
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Cumulative frequency curves (ogive)
         Content summary: Drawing cumulative frequency curves (ogives) for grouped data.
         Suggested activity: Learners group their class data into classes and draw an ogive on graph paper.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Grouped frequency table with editable frequencies; the cumulative frequency curve redraws as frequencies change. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (d); Essential (d). One lesson, flat anatomy.
         Activity: as planning notes (class journey times grouped and plotted on graph paper; a ready-made table for classes without their own data)
         JSXGraph: fig-ogive-builder (assets/jsxgraph/ogive-builder.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/02-cumulative-frequency-curves/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a cumulative frequency counts every value below an upper class boundary, which is why it is plotted there.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> complete a cumulative frequency column, plot the points and join them.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> read from the curve how many values lie below, or above, a given value.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners build running totals from a grouped table and plot them on graph paper.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Hand out graph paper. Either collect the class's journey times to school and group them on the board in the classes below, or use the table. After part (b), ask: <q>By 14.5 minutes, how many learners have certainly arrived?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-getting-to-school">
        <title>Getting to School</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The table shows the journey times to school, in minutes (to the nearest minute), of <m>40</m> learners in Nakuru.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-getting-to-school">
                <title>Journey Times of 40 Learners</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Time (minutes)</cell><cell>5–14</cell><cell>15–24</cell><cell>25–34</cell><cell>35–44</cell><cell>45–54</cell><cell>55–64</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Add a row of running totals: the number of learners in each class and all the classes before it.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>4, 13, 26, 34, 38, 40</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The first running total, <m>4</m>, belongs at a time on the horizontal axis. Why should it go at <m>14.5</m> minutes, and not at <m>10</m> or <m>14</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Every learner in 5–14 took less than <m>14.5</m> minutes (a time of <m>14.4</m> minutes rounds to <m>14</m>), so by <m>14.5</m> minutes all <m>4</m> have certainly arrived. At <m>10</m> or <m>14</m> we cannot be sure all <m>4</m> have.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Plot the points <m>(4.5, 0)</m>, <m>(14.5, 4)</m>, <m>(24.5, 13)</m>, <m>(34.5, 26)</m>, <m>(44.5, 34)</m>, <m>(54.5, 38)</m> and <m>(64.5, 40)</m> on graph paper.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Use <m>2\,\text{cm}</m> for <m>10</m> minutes and <m>2\,\text{cm}</m> for <m>10</m> learners. Check that the first point is on the axis and the last at <m>40</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Join the points. What shape do you get?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The graph never falls; it rises steeply where the frequencies are large (the class 25–34) and levels off at <m>40</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Use your graph to estimate how many learners took less than <m>30</m> minutes.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>20</m> learners. By calculation along the straight segment: <m>13 + 13 \times \dfrac{30 - 24.5}{10} = 20.15</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise cumulative frequency and the rules for plotting a cumulative frequency curve.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (b) is the reason for the upper class boundary.</p></li>
                <li><p>Start at the first lower class boundary with cumulative frequency <m>0</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The graph never falls, and its last point is at the total frequency <m>n</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-cumulative-frequency-curve">
        <title>Cumulative Frequency Curves</title>
        <p>
            The <term>cumulative frequency</term> of a class is its frequency added to the frequencies of all the classes before it. The cumulative frequency at an upper class boundary counts every value below that boundary.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <term>cumulative frequency curve</term>, or <term>ogive</term>, plots each cumulative frequency at the upper class boundary of its class. It starts at the lower class boundary of the first class, with cumulative frequency <m>0</m>, and ends at the total frequency <m>n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            In this book the points are joined with straight line segments, so readings from a graph agree with calculations. When you draw an ogive by hand you may join the points with a smooth curve instead; the readings are almost the same.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ogive-builder">
        <caption>Drag a frequency slider. The cumulative frequencies and the curve change. The last point always sits at the total frequency.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ogive-builder" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/ogive-builder.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-ogive-builder" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>The sliders start at the journey times from <xref ref="act-getting-to-school"/>. Drag the <alert>5–14</alert> slider and watch every point of the curve move up or down.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the <alert>55–64</alert> slider: only the last point moves.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Make one class much larger than the others. Where is the curve steepest?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <warning xml:id="warning-ogive-midpoints">
        <title>Not at the Midpoint</title>
        <p>
            Plotting each cumulative frequency at the class midpoint moves the whole curve half a class to the left, so every value read from it is too small. Plot at the upper class boundary.
        </p>
    </warning>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-goats-ogive">
        <title>Goats at Garissa Market</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The masses, in kg (to the nearest kg), of <m>50</m> goats at Garissa livestock market are shown below. Draw a cumulative frequency curve.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-goats">
                <title>Masses of 50 Goats</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Mass (kg)</cell><cell>15–19</cell><cell>20–24</cell><cell>25–29</cell><cell>30–34</cell><cell>35–39</cell><cell>40–44</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The masses are to the nearest kg, so the class 15–19 runs from <m>14.5</m> to <m>19.5\,\text{kg}</m>, and so on.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-goats-cf">
                <title>Cumulative Frequencies of the Goat Masses</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Class</cell><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>Upper class boundary</cell><cell>Cumulative frequency (cf)</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>15–19</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>19.5</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>20–24</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>24.5</cell><cell>10</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>25–29</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>29.5</cell><cell>22</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>30–34</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>34.5</cell><cell>36</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>35–39</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>39.5</cell><cell>45</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>40–44</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>44.5</cell><cell>50</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>Plot <m>(14.5, 0)</m> and then each upper class boundary against its cumulative frequency, and join the points.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-goats-ogive">
                <caption>Cumulative frequency curve of the masses of 50 goats.</caption>
                <image width="90%">
                    <shortdescription>Cumulative frequency of goat masses: points (14.5, 0), (19.5, 3), (24.5, 10), (29.5, 22), (34.5, 36), (39.5, 45), (44.5, 50) joined by straight lines.</shortdescription>
                    <description><p>Horizontal axis mass in kg from 10 to 50; vertical axis cumulative frequency from 0 to 50. Plotted points: (14.5, 0), (19.5, 3), (24.5, 10), (29.5, 22), (34.5, 36), (39.5, 45), (44.5, 50), joined by straight line segments.</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=10, xmax=50, ymin=0, ymax=52,
  xtick={10,15,...,50}, ytick={0,10,...,50}, minor x tick num=4, minor y tick num=4,
  grid=both, major grid style={gray!50}, minor grid style={gray!20},
  xlabel={Mass (kg)}, ylabel={Cumulative frequency}]
\addplot[thick, mark=*] coordinates {(14.5,0) (19.5,3) (24.5,10) (29.5,22) (34.5,36) (39.5,45) (44.5,50)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-bank-ogive">
        <title>Waiting at the Bank</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The waiting times of <m>50</m> customers at a bank in Kisumu are shown below. Draw a cumulative frequency curve and use it to estimate how many customers waited less than <m>12</m> minutes, and how many waited more than <m>12</m> minutes.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-bank">
                <title>Waiting Times of 50 Customers</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Time, <m>t</m> (minutes)</cell><cell><m>0 \le t \lt 5</m></cell><cell><m>5 \le t \lt 10</m></cell><cell><m>10 \le t \lt 15</m></cell><cell><m>15 \le t \lt 20</m></cell><cell><m>20 \le t \lt 25</m></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>These classes are written with their boundaries, so the upper class boundaries are <m>5, 10, 15, 20, 25</m>. The cumulative frequencies are <m>6, 20, 38, 46, 50</m>, so the points are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(5, 6)</m>, <m>(10, 20)</m>, <m>(15, 38)</m>, <m>(20, 46)</m> and <m>(25, 50)</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-bank-ogive">
                <caption>Cumulative frequency curve of the waiting times, with the reading at 12 minutes.</caption>
                <image width="90%">
                    <shortdescription>Cumulative frequency of waiting times: points (0, 0), (5, 6), (10, 20), (15, 38), (20, 46), (25, 50) joined by straight lines. A dashed line up from 12 minutes meets the curve at about 27 customers.</shortdescription>
                    <description><p>Horizontal axis time in minutes from 0 to 25; vertical axis cumulative frequency from 0 to 50. Plotted points: (0, 0), (5, 6), (10, 20), (15, 38), (20, 46), (25, 50), joined by straight line segments. A dashed vertical line from 12 on the time axis meets the graph at height 27.2, and a dashed horizontal line goes from there to the cumulative frequency axis.</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=0, xmax=26, ymin=0, ymax=52,
  xtick={0,5,...,25}, ytick={0,10,...,50}, minor x tick num=4, minor y tick num=4,
  grid=both, major grid style={gray!50}, minor grid style={gray!20},
  xlabel={Waiting time (minutes)}, ylabel={Cumulative frequency}]
\addplot[thick, mark=*] coordinates {(0,0) (5,6) (10,20) (15,38) (20,46) (25,50)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(12,0) (12,27.2) (0,27.2)};
\node[anchor=south west] at (axis cs:0.3,27.6) {about 27};
\node[anchor=north west] at (axis cs:12.2,4) {12};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>Go up from <m>12</m> minutes to the graph and across: about <m>27</m> customers waited less than <m>12</m> minutes. (By calculation along the segment from <m>(10, 20)</m> to <m>(15, 38)</m>: <m>20 + 18 \times \dfrac{2}{5} = 27.2</m>.) So about <m>50 - 27 = 23</m> customers waited more than <m>12</m> minutes.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-maize-plants-cf">
        <title>From Cumulative Frequency Back to Frequency</title>
        <statement>
            <p>An agricultural officer measured <m>80</m> maize plants and recorded how many were shorter than each height.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-maize-plants">
                <title>Heights of 80 Maize Plants</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Height less than (cm)</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>40</cell><cell>60</cell><cell>80</cell><cell>100</cell><cell>120</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Cumulative frequency</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>17</cell><cell>38</cell><cell>62</cell><cell>74</cell><cell>80</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>Find the number of plants in each class <m>0 \le h \lt 20</m>, <m>20 \le h \lt 40</m>, and so on.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Each frequency is a cumulative frequency minus the one before it: <m>5</m>, <m>17 - 5 = 12</m>, <m>38 - 17 = 21</m>, <m>62 - 38 = 24</m>, <m>74 - 62 = 12</m> and <m>80 - 74 = 6</m>. Check: <m>5 + 12 + 21 + 24 + 12 + 6 = 80</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

