    <exercises xml:id="exercises-cumulative-frequency-curves">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ogive-seedlings">
            <statement>
                <p>The heights, in cm (to the nearest cm), of <m>60</m> seedlings at a tree nursery in Kakamega are shown below. Complete a cumulative frequency column, write down the points to plot, and draw the cumulative frequency curve. Keep your graph: you will use it again in <xref ref="sec-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data"/> and <xref ref="sec-measures-of-dispersion"/>.</p>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Height (cm)</cell><cell>10–14</cell><cell>15–19</cell><cell>20–24</cell><cell>25–29</cell><cell>30–34</cell><cell>35–39</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
            <answer><p>cf <m>4, 13, 28, 46, 56, 60</m>; points <m>(9.5, 0)</m>, <m>(14.5, 4)</m>, <m>(19.5, 13)</m>, <m>(24.5, 28)</m>, <m>(29.5, 46)</m>, <m>(34.5, 56)</m>, <m>(39.5, 60)</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ogive-rainfall">
            <statement>
                <p>The daily rainfall, <m>r</m> mm, on <m>40</m> wet days at Kericho is shown below. Write down the points to plot and draw the cumulative frequency curve.</p>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Rainfall (mm)</cell><cell><m>0 \le r \lt 10</m></cell><cell><m>10 \le r \lt 20</m></cell><cell><m>20 \le r \lt 30</m></cell><cell><m>30 \le r \lt 40</m></cell><cell><m>40 \le r \lt 50</m></cell><cell><m>50 \le r \lt 60</m></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
            <answer><p><m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(10, 5)</m>, <m>(20, 13)</m>, <m>(30, 25)</m>, <m>(40, 34)</m>, <m>(50, 38)</m>, <m>(60, 40)</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ogive-reading-counts">
            <statement><p>Use your table and graph from <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/>. How many seedlings are shorter than <m>24.5\,\text{cm}</m>? How many are <m>29.5\,\text{cm}</m> or taller? Estimate how many are shorter than <m>27\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>28</m>; <m>14</m>; about <m>37</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The cumulative frequency at <m>24.5</m> is <m>28</m>. At <m>29.5</m> it is <m>46</m>, so <m>60 - 46 = 14</m> are <m>29.5\,\text{cm}</m> or taller. Going up from <m>27</m>: <m>28 + 18 \times \dfrac{27 - 24.5}{5} = 37</m>, so about <m>37</m> seedlings.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ogive-back-to-frequency">
            <statement><p>The cumulative frequencies for the classes 10–19, 20–29, 30–39, 40–49, 50–59 and 60–69 are <m>3, 11, 26, 44, 54, 60</m>. Find the frequencies.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>3, 8, 15, 18, 10, 6</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-juma-ogive">
            <statement><p>Juma drew a cumulative frequency curve for the goat masses in <xref ref="example-goats-ogive"/>, but he plotted the cumulative frequencies <m>3, 10, 22, 36, 45, 50</m> at <m>17, 22, 27, 32, 37, 42</m>. What did he do wrong, and how does it affect a median read from his graph?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>He plotted at the class midpoints. His curve is half a class (<m>2.5\,\text{kg}</m>) too far left, so his median is about <m>2.5\,\text{kg}</m> too small.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>17, 22, \ldots</m> are the midpoints of the classes, not the upper class boundaries <m>19.5, 24.5, \ldots</m>. Every point is <m>2.5\,\text{kg}</m> to the left of where it should be, so any value read across from the cumulative frequency axis, including the median, comes out about <m>2.5\,\text{kg}</m> too small.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-curves-one-grid">
            <title>Challenge: Two Curves on One Grid</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The heights, in cm (to the nearest cm), of <m>30</m> boys and <m>30</m> girls in a Grade 12 class are shown below. Draw both cumulative frequency curves on one grid and compare the two groups.</p>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Height (cm)</cell><cell>150–154</cell><cell>155–159</cell><cell>160–164</cell><cell>165–169</cell><cell>170–174</cell><cell>175–179</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Boys</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Girls</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
            <hint><p>Read both curves at <m>164.5\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>Boys' cf <m>2, 7, 16, 24, 28, 30</m>; girls' cf <m>4, 12, 22, 27, 29, 30</m>. The girls' curve lies to the left, so the girls are generally shorter: <m>22</m> girls but only <m>16</m> boys are shorter than <m>164.5\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-cumulative-frequency-curves">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide axes already drawn with scales, and a cumulative frequency column with the first two entries filled in. Assign <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/> and <xref ref="ex-ogive-back-to-frequency"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/> to <xref ref="ex-juma-ogive"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-two-curves-one-grid"/>. Extension: which class gives the steepest part of the curve, and why? (Answer: the class with the largest frequency, here the modal class, because the most values are added there.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
</section>
